  <h2>First, check you are comparing to the right people</h2>
  <p>"Weak" is relative. The <a href="/bench-press-standards/">bench press standards</a> page shows percentiles from real powerlifting results, and separate working benchmarks for people who train but do not compete. Check where your number sits before deciding something is wrong. If you are actually stalled, the rest of this page helps.</p>

  <h2>The four usual causes</h2>
  <div class="chart-wrap">
    <table class="pct-table data-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Cause</th><th>What it looks like</th><th>How to test it</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Technique</td><td class="wrap">The <a href="/bench-press-technique/bar-path/">bar path</a> wobbles, your body shifts during the set, the reps are not smooth or repeatable.</td><td class="wrap">Film a set and do paused reps. If form breaks down well below your max, technique is the limit.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Recovery</td><td class="wrap">Poor sleep, training feels like a grind, you feel worn out, and your numbers drift down or stay flat.</td><td class="wrap">Run a deload week and see if the bar moves better afterward.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Programming</td><td class="wrap">The same sets, reps and weights for months, or a new program every two weeks.</td><td class="wrap">Look back at your log. Has the weight or the reps gone up, or has it all stayed the same?</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Leverage</td><td class="wrap">The bar slows at the same spot on every heavy rep.</td><td class="wrap">Film from the side and note where the bar slows: off the chest, midway, or near lockout.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The symptoms and tests in this table are general coaching practice. The points below come from Jordan's coaching interview and from the research.</p>

  <h2>What Jordan checks first</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Technique, for newer lifters.</strong> Jordan says that when a beginner stalls it is usually a form problem: not positioning the body correctly, not using the right muscles in the right order, or movements that are not coordinated and smooth.</li>
    <li><strong>Recovery and injuries, for everyone else.</strong> Asked why a squat stops going up, he named three things first: being overtrained, being under-recovered, or an underlying injury the lifter does not realize is holding them back. He says the nervous system is generally what is fatigued.</li>
    <li><strong>Programming.</strong> If the same load is used forever, there is no new stress to adapt to. His first fix is to add reps, then add weight. When adding weight every week stops working, he slows the lowering phase or adds a 3 second pause at the bottom.</li>
    <li><strong>Patience.</strong> He suggests 8 to 12 weeks on a first program, because progress early on is mostly neurological and the muscle takes longer to respond.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The sticking region</h2>
  <p>Most lifters notice the bar slows at some point on a heavy rep. That point is called the sticking region, and two studies from the same research group help explain it.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>It shows up even without the stretch.</strong> van den Tillaar and Ettema tested 11 men on a one rep max bench press done normally and as a pure concentric lift, with no lowering phase before the press. A sticking region showed up in both. The authors concluded that fading stretch-shortening help from the lowering phase does not explain it, and that it is more likely a poor mechanical position.</li>
    <li><strong>Where it appears changes with your grip.</strong> Gomo and van den Tillaar had 12 experienced powerlifters do one rep maxes with narrow, medium and wide grips. Everyone had a clear sticking region with all three, but it did not occur at the same elbow and shoulder angles from one grip to the next. The authors point to differences in how far the bar sits from the elbow joint.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>What that means in practice: a sticking point is partly built into the lift, and it is not only a sign of a weak muscle. Where yours sits depends on your body and your grip. If the bar always slows in the same spot, grip width is one thing you can test, but make that your only change for a few weeks, so you know what did it.</p>

  <h2>A simple order to work in</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Film a heavy set</strong> from the side and from behind your head, and note where the bar slows.</li>
    <li><strong>Rule out fatigue.</strong> Take a deload week (see <a href="/how-to-deload-bench-press-properly/">how to deload</a>) and test again.</li>
    <li><strong>Change one thing.</strong> A grip adjustment, a pause, a slower lowering phase, or a different weekly structure. Not all four.</li>
    <li><strong>Give it 8 to 12 weeks</strong> before you judge it, and keep a log so you can see what changed.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What not to do</h2>
  <p>Do not read soreness as a sign of a good session. Jordan describes it as a byproduct of working out, not a predictor of progress. And do not try to progress too fast: he lists not over-progressing yourself among the things he would tell any new lifter.</p>
